OpenTTD @VERSION@ for Plan 9: the source (9front and 9legacy, amd64)

This is OpenTTD, the free transport simulation game, as C that
Plan 9's own compilers build: 6c and 6l, on 9front or on 9legacy
(Plan 9 from Bell Labs, 4th edition, with the 9legacy patches).
Nothing here needs a C++ compiler.

The C in tu is generated

OpenTTD is written in C++.  The C in tu was not written by anyone:
an automated pipeline translated OpenTTD's own source into it.  It
is complete and builds as it is, but it is a compiler's output, as
an assembler listing would be: long, flat and hard to read, with
names from LLVM (_1234, llvm_cbe_...) and from C++'s name mangling
(_ZN11VideoDriver4TickEv).  Do not edit it to change the game:
change OpenTTD's source and translate it again (below), or change
the runtime, which is hand-written.

How it was made (https://github.com/yrk-lab/ottd-p9, commit
@COMMIT@):

1. OpenTTD @VERSION@ (@OTTDHASH@), with a few patches for Plan 9:
   the video, sound and music drivers, Plan 9's paths, web links
   through the plumber.
2. Each of its source files compiled by clang to LLVM IR (@CLANG@),
   for amd64, with libc++ and libc++abi (LLVM @LLVM@) as IR too, and
   zlib (@ZLIB@) and liblzma (xz @XZ@) translated with it.
3. Exceptions lowered: the C that comes out has no unwinding, so
   calls that may throw go through small wrappers (tu/invoke.c) that
   return what was thrown, as the runtime's exception support
   (runtime/eh) expects.
4. llvm-cbe (@CBE@) turned each unit's IR into C, and a lowering
   pass made that C acceptable to Plan 9's compilers: tu/*.c, one
   file per OpenTTD source file, 600 or so; tu/ctors.c runs the
   static constructors.
5. The C library calls the program makes are renamed (c_...), so
   that the runtime answers them, on Plan 9's library.

The rest is hand-written, and meant to be read and changed:

	runtime/plan9	the runtime: the C library the game expects
			(stdio, files, time, errno, glibc's data layouts),
			threads as Plan 9 processes, sockets on /net,
			notes as signals, the draw, keyboard and audio
			shims under the game's Plan 9 drivers; files
			ending in 9f are 9front's, others are shared or
			9legacy's (Plan 9 from Bell Labs carried on)
	runtime/eh	the exception runtime the lowering targets
	tu/mkfile	generated too, but plain mk: config.9front and
			config.9legacy say which runtime parts each
			system takes

FreeType (freetype) is not translated: it is FreeType @FREETYPE@, its own C, with
its Plan 9 mkfile, built here and linked in.  The data (lang, ai,
game, media, bin, baseset, with OpenGFX, OpenSFX and OpenMSX) are
the game's.

Build and install it

	mk

builds FreeType, then the game (a few minutes; the link is the
longest step), and leaves 6.openttd here, next to its data: run it
as ./6.openttd from a rio window or on the bare screen.  The system
is found from libc.h (9front has atomic(2)); mk SYS=9front or
SYS=9legacy says it.  Then

	mk install

for yourself: $home/bin/$cputype/openttd, the data in
$home/lib/openttd.  For everyone (as the host owner):

	mk BIN=/$cputype/bin install

with the data in /sys/lib/openttd.  mk uninstall takes it away;
mk clean the objects.

Differences between the systems

9front: sound through /dev/audio, music through games/dmid and
games/opl3, keys going down and up (/dev/kbd).  9legacy: sound where
there is a device (not on amd64), no MIDI player (no music), keys as
characters (a held arrow does not scroll).

Translating it again

The repository (https://github.com/yrk-lab/ottd-p9) has the
pipeline: phase1/setup.sh fetches OpenTTD, the base sets, zlib, xz
and FreeType; phase1/build_p9.sh and phase1/translate.sh make the
IR; phase2/buildtu.sh makes the C in tu; phase6/srcbundle.sh makes
this bundle.  They run on Linux (Debian or Ubuntu, clang 17).

Licences

OpenTTD: GNU General Public License, version 2 (doc/COPYING.md),
and its third-party code's (doc/3rdparty-*).  The translated libc++
and libc++abi: Apache 2.0 with LLVM exceptions (doc/libcxx-*,
doc/libcxxabi-*).  zlib (doc/zlib-LICENSE), liblzma
(doc/xz-COPYING*), FreeType (doc/FreeType-FTL.TXT).  The base sets
carry their own (baseset/*/license.txt).  OpenTTD's README.md is
here too.
